#!/bin/sh
# UMS pre-push guard (Publication Contract) v2
#
# This is the actual enforcement boundary for the Publication Contract (see
# UMS_MEMORY_BANK_CONTRACT.md, "Publication Contract"). Once the gate below
# decides the push is worth judging at all, it judges a push to a protected
# branch by its CONTENT rather than by branch name alone: a push is let
# through when it is a fast-forward onto commits already reachable from this
# clone's own remote-tracking refs for the remote being pushed to
# (is_integration_push, below) — evidence of publication, not a record of
# who reviewed the branch. That evidence is only as honest as those
# remote-tracking refs (see is_integration_push's own comment). Unlike a
# PreToolUse hook working from a shell command string, git invokes this hook
# with fully-resolved refs on stdin — every CLI spelling trick (HEAD,
# --git-dir, bash -c, env prefixes, operator adjacency, config overrides)
# collapses to the same four fields below, so there is no shell syntax left
# to bypass.
#
# GATED ON AN AGENT-SESSION MARKER: everything below the stdin buffer only
# fires when is_agent_session() finds MB_AGENT_SESSION=1 (injected by this
# layer's own settings.json into every harness it configures), or — Claude
# Code only — a non-empty AI_AGENT or CLAUDECODE=1 as a fallback. Without the
# marker this hook enforces none of its OWN checks: it delegates straight to
# the chained foreign hook (run_chained) and exits with its result. The
# fail-closed stdin buffer above still applies to everyone regardless of the
# marker (an unwritable TMPDIR still rejects a human's push, deliberately —
# see its own comment), so this gate narrows what the hook enforces, not
# whether it can ever reject a push at all. The actor rule ("the moment of
# integration belongs to a human") lives in the harness-side PreToolUse
# guard, not here — a git hook cannot itself tell a human from an agent, so
# this file's job is narrower: stay out of a human's way in the ordinary
# case of a terminal or IDE push outside an agent session.
#
# Scope: refs/heads/* (branches) only. The content rule is branch-scoped; tag
# pushes and anything else are out of scope of it (a tag delete or a tag
# force-move is not a "branch push") and reach no check below the scope gate.
# They are NOT untouched, though: the fail-closed stdin-buffer arm above that
# scope gate still applies to them, so a push that includes a tag ref rejects
# for everyone, marker or not, if that arm cannot buffer the ref list.
#
# Installed per clone by ums/.claude/hooks/install-git-hooks.ps1, resolved
# via `git rev-parse --git-path hooks/pre-push` so this lands where git
# ACTUALLY looks even for a linked worktree or a core.hooksPath override
# (git hooks live outside version control, so this guarantee only exists
# once that script has been run against a given working copy).
#
# THE HUMAN ESCAPE: MB_HUMAN_PUSH=1 in the environment means "a human takes
# responsibility for THIS push" and lifts the WHOLE guard — the protected-
# branch rule, the deletion ban and the force-push ban alike. It exists
# because a git hook cannot tell a human from an agent: the whole layer asks
# the USER to publish a protected branch themselves, so without an escape the
# hook would block the very remedy it points at, and `--no-verify` — which
# disables every hook, not just one rule — would become the only way out. The
# WIDE scope is deliberate, not sloppiness: now that everything here is gated
# on the agent-session marker, a human who rebases their OWN ticket branch
# in-session carries that marker too, and a narrow escape would leave them
# nothing but turning hooks off entirely. UMS_ALLOW_SHARED_PUSH=1 is the old
# name, still honoured for the transition with a deprecation line on stderr.
# The rejection message below names the escape, so whoever hits the wall
# learns the way through it at that moment. An AGENT MUST NEVER SET IT: that
# is contract text (Publication Contract), the same trust model as every other
# rule of this layer, not something this hook can enforce — the mechanical
# containment lives in the PreToolUse guard (guard-git-push.mjs), which denies
# any command carrying either name.
#
# Two known, accepted bypasses — both require deliberate, visible intent,
# unlike every CLI-spelling trick this hook exists to close:
#   - `git push --no-verify` skips this hook entirely.
#   - a one-shot `git -c core.hooksPath=<other> push` points git at a hooks
#     directory this layer never installed into.
#
# A CONFIGURED core.hooksPath (local or global, routine with husky or
# pre-commit) is NOT a bypass and must never be reported as one: the installer
# detects it, installs where git actually looks and proves the hook live there,
# and every check in the layer resolves the hook through
# `git rev-parse --git-path hooks/pre-push`, which honours it. What remains is
# provenance and scope, settled by the `UMS pre-push guard` marker below — see
# UMS_MEMORY_BANK_CONTRACT.md, the paragraph on a configured core.hooksPath at
# the end of the Publication Contract's push-policy discussion.
#
# Invocation: $1 = remote name, $2 = remote URL. Stdin lines:
#   <local ref> SP <local sha1> SP <remote ref> SP <remote sha1> LF

remote_name="$1"
zero='0000000000000000000000000000000000000000'
reject=0

# Git hands the hook exactly ONE stdin. To also pass it to a chained hook
# (typically `git lfs pre-push`, which reads the same ref list), it has to be
# set aside first. A temp FILE, not command substitution: per the playbook
# that is not a transparent channel and behaves differently under msys than
# under a real POSIX shell. This is the ONLY thing on this file's own raw
# stdin - every other subcommand below runs after the buffer exists, so
# nothing else here can ever swallow ref lines by accident.
#
# Fail CLOSED, not open: if the buffer cannot be created (unwritable/
# unresolvable TMPDIR), the main loop below would read from a missing file,
# process no refs, leave `reject` at 0 and fall through to an allowed push -
# this is the layer's enforcement boundary, so a plumbing failure here must
# reject the push, not silently skip every check.
stdin_buf="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/ums-prepush-$$"
cat > "$stdin_buf" || {
    echo "UMS: nelze odložit stdin do $stdin_buf - push zamítnut (Publication Contract)." >&2
    exit 1
}
trap 'rm -f "$stdin_buf"' EXIT INT TERM

# Directory this hook was actually run from - also correct under
# core.hooksPath and in a linked worktree. Resolved absolutely, because the
# hook's cwd is the repository root, not the hook's own directory.
hooks_dir=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")" && pwd)
chained="$hooks_dir/pre-push.ums-chained"

run_chained() {
    [ -x "$chained" ] || return 0
    "$chained" "$@" < "$stdin_buf"
}

# This layer injects the marker itself into every harness's own
# configuration. AI_AGENT/CLAUDECODE are a fallback for Claude Code only -
# someone else owns those variables, and other harnesses do not have them
# at all.
is_agent_session() {
    [ "$MB_AGENT_SESSION" = "1" ] && return 0
    [ -n "$AI_AGENT" ] && return 0
    [ "$CLAUDECODE" = "1" ] && return 0
    return 1
}

# A human in a terminal or IDE is not meant to notice this hook exists. NOT
# a plain `exit 0`: the chained foreign hook (LFS) still has to run, or
# installing this layer would silently turn LFS off for everyone on the
# team.
if ! is_agent_session; then
    run_chained "$@"
    exit $?
fi

# The single unified way out for a human: it lifts the WHOLE guard, not one
# rule (see THE HUMAN ESCAPE in the header for why the scope is this wide).
# Decided HERE, above every check, because the decision is about WHO is
# pushing, not about what a particular ref does. Per the contract an agent
# never sets it; the mechanical containment against one that does is the
# PreToolUse guard, not a narrower scope here.
human_escape=0
if [ "$MB_HUMAN_PUSH" = "1" ]; then
    human_escape=1
elif [ "$UMS_ALLOW_SHARED_PUSH" = "1" ]; then
    human_escape=1
    echo "UMS: UMS_ALLOW_SHARED_PUSH je zastaralé jméno — používej MB_HUMAN_PUSH=1." >&2
fi

# Announced, never silent: an escape left set in a shell would otherwise
# disarm the guarantee invisibly for the rest of that shell's life. NOT a
# plain `exit 0` — the chained foreign hook (LFS) still has to run.
if [ "$human_escape" = "1" ]; then
    echo "UMS: MB_HUMAN_PUSH=1 — vědomá výjimka člověka, guard je pro tenhle push zvednutý." >&2
    run_chained "$@"
    exit $?
fi

branch_name() {
    # Prefix stripped case-insensitively, so the name printed in the message
    # is right for any spelling the scope gate below let through.
    printf '%s' "$1" | sed 's#^[Rr][Ee][Ff][Ss]/[Hh][Ee][Aa][Dd][Ss]/##'
}

to_lower() {
    printf '%s' "$1" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]'
}

# Protected-branch patterns come from the repository configuration
# (contract: "Repository Configuration"), materialized as one glob per line
# by install-git-hooks.ps1 — this is POSIX sh, there is no JSON parser here.
# A missing, empty or comment-only file falls back to the built-in list:
# degradation must always lead to MORE protection, never less.
#
# Loaded ONCE, BEFORE the main loop: the loop reads git's ref list from the
# hook's stdin, and any stdin-consuming command inside it would swallow the
# remaining refs — a hook that silently stops checking looks exactly like a
# hook that works.
common_dir=$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null)
[ -z "$common_dir" ] && common_dir='.git'
protected_file="$common_dir/ums-protected-branches"

protected_patterns=''
if [ -f "$protected_file" ]; then
    # CR is deleted along with blanks, and that is load-bearing, not tidiness:
    # a generator running on Windows easily writes the list with CRLF, and a
    # NON-msys sh (Linux, macOS, WSL bash on a shared checkout) converts
    # nothing on the way in. Every pattern would become `develop\r`, the list
    # would be NON-empty so the fallback never fires, nothing would match, and
    # the repository would be fully unprotected with hook and configuration
    # both looking correct — the same inverted degradation `set -f` prevents.
    protected_patterns=$(sed -e 's/#.*//' "$protected_file" \
        | tr -d '[:blank:]\r' \
        | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]' \
        | grep -v '^$')
fi
if [ -z "$protected_patterns" ]; then
    protected_patterns='develop
main
master
release/*'
fi

# Pathname expansion off for the rest of this hook. The `for` below relies on
# an unquoted expansion to split the pattern list on whitespace — and an
# unquoted expansion in POSIX sh is ALSO glob-expanded against the working
# tree, which is the repository root when git runs a hook. Measured, not
# theorized: in a clone that has a top-level `branches/` directory, the
# pattern `branches/*` came out of the loop as `branches/notes.txt` and the
# branch was pushed through unprotected. `release/*` has the same exposure.
# Nothing else in this hook globs ($pat inside `case` is a pattern, not an
# expansion, and is unaffected by -f).
set -f

# $pat is deliberately UNQUOTED inside `case` so it is treated as a glob.
is_protected() {
    for pat in $protected_patterns; do
        case "$1" in
            $pat) return 0 ;;
        esac
    done
    return 1
}

# Integration = moving a protected branch's pointer onto commits reachable
# from THIS CLONE's OWN remote-tracking refs for the remote being pushed to
# (refs/remotes/$remote_name/*, checked below via `git for-each-ref`) — not
# from the remote itself, which this hook never contacts. That is evidence
# of publication, not of review: an agent may push its own ticket branch, so
# it can manufacture that reachability itself. The actor rule (who is
# allowed to make the moment of integration happen) lives in the
# PreToolUse guard, not here — see the Publication Contract. Being local
# refs, this evidence is only as honest as they are: it trusts that nothing
# in this clone wrote or left behind a remote-tracking ref that does not
# reflect what is actually on the remote (see the header's own caveat).
#
# Both subcommands close their own stdin - they run inside the main loop,
# which reads git's ref list from the same stdin buffer, and any
# stdin-consuming command in there would silently swallow the remaining refs.
is_integration_push() {
    _local="$1"
    _remote="$2"
    [ "$_local" = "$zero" ] && return 1
    [ "$_remote" = "$zero" ] && return 1
    git merge-base --is-ancestor "$_remote" "$_local" </dev/null 2>/dev/null || return 1
    _refs=$(git for-each-ref --contains "$_local" --format='%(refname)' \
        "refs/remotes/$remote_name/" </dev/null 2>/dev/null)
    [ -n "$_refs" ]
}

while read -r local_ref local_sha remote_ref remote_sha; do
    [ -z "$local_ref" ] && continue

    # Everything below matches against the LOWER-CASED ref. The scope gate
    # and the protected-name check are two halves of one rule, so they must
    # agree about case: a case-sensitive gate let `refs/HEADS/develop` skip
    # the guard entirely, even though the check below would have caught it.
    # (guard-git-push.mjs is case-insensitive here too - the layers must not
    # disagree either.)
    remote_ref_lc=$(to_lower "$remote_ref")

    # Out of scope: only refs/heads/* (branches) are policed by this loop's
    # own checks. Tags and anything else skip straight to the next ref.
    case "$remote_ref_lc" in
        refs/heads/*) ;;
        *) continue ;;
    esac

    branch_lc=${remote_ref_lc#refs/heads/}

    # Judged BEFORE the protected-branch rule, and the order is the whole
    # point of this position. A delete sends a zero local sha, which makes
    # is_integration_push return false, so with the protected rule first a
    # `git push origin --delete develop` was rejected with the shared-branch
    # message - which advises publishing the branch and handing over
    # `git push origin HEAD:develop`, advice about a push the user never
    # attempted, while the message that actually describes a delete was
    # unreachable for exactly the branches where it matters most. The verdict
    # was right either way; only the text misled.
    if [ "$local_sha" = "$zero" ]; then
        b=$(branch_name "$remote_ref")
        echo "UMS: mazání větve '$b' přes push není povolené (Publication Contract)." >&2
        reject=1
        continue
    fi

    if is_protected "$branch_lc"; then
        b=$(branch_name "$remote_ref")
        if is_integration_push "$local_sha" "$remote_sha"; then
            echo "UMS: '$b' — fast-forward na commity už publikované na '$remote_name', push povolen." >&2
        else
            echo "UMS: '$b' je sdílená větev — projde jen fast-forward na commity, které už jsou" >&2
            echo "     na '$remote_name' (Publication Contract). Nejdřív publikuj svoji větev." >&2
            echo "     Připrav příkaz a nech ho uživateli:" >&2
            # Refspec form on purpose: in a ticket clone WITHOUT a local
            # copy of the shared branch, `git push origin <b>` fails on an
            # unknown local ref, so the advice would not even run.
            echo "     \`! MB_HUMAN_PUSH=1 git push $remote_name HEAD:$b\`" >&2
            echo "     MB_HUMAN_PUSH=1 je vědomá výjimka pro člověka; agent ji nikdy nenastavuje." >&2
            echo "     (Výjimka zvedá celý guard: člověk za tenhle jeden push přebírá odpovědnost.)" >&2
            reject=1
            continue
        fi
    fi

    if [ "$remote_sha" != "$zero" ]; then
        # stdin closed for the same reason is_integration_push closes it on
        # both of its subcommands: this runs inside the loop that reads git's
        # ref list, and anything stdin-consuming in here silently swallows the
        # remaining refs. Harmless for THIS subcommand today, but the
        # invariant is stated one function up and has to be applied wherever
        # it holds, not only where it currently bites.
        if ! git merge-base --is-ancestor "$remote_sha" "$local_sha" </dev/null 2>/dev/null; then
            b=$(branch_name "$remote_ref")
            echo "UMS: push do '$b' není fast-forward (vynucený/force push) — zakázáno" >&2
            echo "     (Publication Contract). Historii nikdy nepřepisuj tiše, řeš to s uživatelem." >&2
            # Non-fast-forward has two very different causes; naming the benign
            # one keeps a routine "the base moved" from being read as an
            # attempted history rewrite.
            echo "     Pokud integruješ tiketovou větev, báze se nejspíš pohnula: udělej" >&2
            echo "     git fetch a merge origin/<báze>, znovu ověř a zkus push ještě jednou." >&2
            reject=1
            continue
        fi
    fi
done < "$stdin_buf"

# Does not run when this hook is rejecting the push: the push will not
# happen anyway, so a chained hook has nothing to do (LFS would upload
# objects for nothing).
if [ "$reject" -ne 0 ]; then
    exit "$reject"
fi

run_chained "$@"
exit $?
